<% content_for :title, "NavishAI — The evaluation lab for AI in technical support" %>
<% content_for :head do %>
  <meta name="description" content="Turn your company's support conversations, policies and expert judgment into source-backed scenarios, calibrated checks and regression tests for AI support systems.">
<% end %>
<% content_for :public_nav do %>
  <nav class="landing-nav" aria-label="On this page" data-turbo="false">
    <%= link_to "How it works", "#workflow" %>
    <%= link_to "Example", "#example" %>
    <%= link_to "Data boundaries", "#privacy" %>
  </nav>
<% end %>
<% content_for :public_actions do %>
  <%= mail_to "hello@navishai.com", "Request a pilot", subject: "NavishAI pilot", class: "button button-small button-primary hide-narrow" %>
<% end %>
<main class="landing">
  <section class="landing-hero" aria-labelledby="landing-title">
    <h1 id="landing-title">The evaluation lab for AI in technical support</h1>
    <p class="landing-lead">Turn your support conversations and playbooks into tests. Your experts approve what good support looks like. Run every new version of your AI agent against them before it reaches customers.</p>
    <div class="landing-actions">
      <%= mail_to "hello@navishai.com", "Request a pilot", subject: "NavishAI pilot", class: "button button-primary button-large" %>
      <%= link_to "How it works", "#workflow", class: "button button-large", data: { turbo: false } %>
    </div>
    <p class="landing-note">NavishAI evaluates your support AI. It never replies to your customers.</p>
    <% if FirstOwnerBootstrap.available? %>
      <p class="landing-note">Setting up this deployment? <%= link_to "First-time setup", new_setup_path %> requires the operator's bootstrap token.</p>
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <figure class="landing-preview" aria-labelledby="preview-caption">
    <div class="preview-window">
      <div class="preview-sidebar" aria-hidden="true">
        <p class="preview-workspace"><span>SQ</span>Support quality</p>
        <% %w[Sources Scenarios Graders Calibration Eval\ suites Runs Trace\ discovery].each do |item| %>
          <span class="<%= "is-current" if item == "Runs" %>"><%= item %></span>
        <% end %>
      </div>
      <div class="preview-main">
        <div class="preview-heading">
          <div><p>Runs / Run 14</p><h2>Replay readiness · candidate v3</h2></div>
          <span class="status-pill status-fail">1 failed</span>
        </div>
        <table class="preview-table">
          <caption class="visually-hidden">Case results for an illustrative run</caption>
          <thead class="visually-hidden"><tr><th scope="col">Result</th><th scope="col">Case</th><th scope="col">Importance</th><th scope="col">Checks</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <% [ [ "fail", "Fail", "Webhook replay writes duplicate invoices", "Critical", "2 checks failed" ],
                 [ "warn", "Abstain", "SSO stopped after certificate rotation", "High", "Judge below threshold" ],
                 [ "pass", "Pass", "REST quota retry headers", "Normal", "3 of 3 checks" ],
                 [ "pass", "Pass", "REST quota retry timing", "Normal", "2 of 2 checks" ],
                 [ "pass", "Pass", "Invoice shows duplicate seat charges", "High", "4 of 4 checks" ] ].each do |tone, result, title, importance, detail| %>
              <tr>
                <td><span class="status-pill status-<%= tone %>"><%= result %></span></td>
                <th scope="row"><%= title %></th>
                <td class="preview-optional"><%= importance %></td>
                <td class="preview-optional"><%= detail %></td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="preview-detail">
        <p class="preview-label">Failed check</p>
        <p class="preview-check">Escalate to Engineering on repeated ACS 500s</p>
        <p class="preview-label">Company evidence</p>
        <blockquote>“Valid IdP metadata plus repeated ACS 500 responses: escalate to Engineering.”</blockquote>
        <p class="preview-source">SSO playbook, section 4.2</p>
        <p class="preview-label">Agent response</p>
        <p>“Please re-upload the metadata and try again.”</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figcaption id="preview-caption">Illustrative data, not customer data or a real run.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section class="landing-section" aria-labelledby="domain-title">
    <div class="landing-section-heading">
      <h2 id="domain-title">Generic benchmarks don't know your product</h2>
      <p>The right answer to an SSO failure, an API error or a billing dispute depends on your playbooks, plans and escalation rules. A polite reply can still be the wrong decision.</p>
    </div>
    <dl class="landing-pillars">
      <div><dt>Built from your evidence</dt><dd>Every scenario links to the exact conversation or document it came from.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Judged by your experts</dt><dd>Experts approve each expected behaviour. Machine proposals stay drafts until they do.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Kept as regressions</dt><dd>A reviewed failure becomes a test for the next version of your agent.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="landing-section" id="workflow" aria-labelledby="workflow-title">
    <div class="landing-section-heading">
      <h2 id="workflow-title">From support history to regression suite</h2>
      <p>Six steps. Each one keeps its sources, its versions and the human decisions behind it. A historical answer is evidence, not automatically the right answer.</p>
    </div>
    <ol class="landing-workflow" role="list">
      <li>
        <h3>Build a source-backed corpus</h3>
        <p>Upload conversation exports and text or Markdown documentation. Fixed snapshots let every scenario point to the exact record it came from.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Find cases worth testing</h3>
        <p>Review proposed issue families, examples and risk signals, and see why each case was picked. Proposals need expert review; selection counts are not verified coverage.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Let experts define good support</h3>
        <p>Correct the situation, facts, expected outcome and escalation rules, then approve an exact version. Controlled variants keep their parent and need their own review.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Compile the behaviour into checks</h3>
        <p>Bind each requirement to a fixed grader and a source excerpt. Deterministic checks inspect reported fields and actions; versioned rubric judges handle context.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Calibrate against human judgment</h3>
        <p>Compare graders with expert labels, inspect false alarms, missed failures and disagreement, and keep held-out evidence separate. Confidence alone is not accuracy.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Inspect failures. Keep regressions.</h3>
        <p>Run a suite against a configured target or a stored output. Read each check beside the response and the evidence, then keep reviewed failures for the next version.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="landing-section" id="example" aria-labelledby="example-title">
    <div class="landing-section-heading">
      <h2 id="example-title">Test the decision, not just the wording</h2>
      <p>One SSO incident can test diagnosis, evidence collection and escalation. This authored example shows the method—not a customer result or a prebuilt SSO integration.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="landing-case">
      <section aria-labelledby="case-title">
        <h3 id="case-title">SAML login fails after an IdP change</h3>
        <dl class="landing-facts">
          <div><dt>Starting context</dt><dd>The customer reports a login failure after updating their identity provider.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Knowledge for the target</dt><dd>The expert chooses which SSO documentation excerpts the agent may see.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Expectation evidence</dt><dd>The company playbook supports the diagnostic steps and handoff rule. Hidden expectations stay out of target input.</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="checks-title">
        <h3 id="checks-title">Checks for this reviewed situation</h3>
        <dl class="landing-checks">
          <div><dt>Collect evidence</dt><dd>Request the certificate expiry date before suggesting a configuration change.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Respect the boundary</dt><dd>Do not claim to have changed the customer's IdP configuration.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Escalate on new facts</dt><dd>When valid metadata still yields repeated ACS 500 responses, hand off to Engineering with the evidence.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Grade with the right tool</dt><dd>A field check can inspect a reported expiry value. A calibrated rubric judge can assess the troubleshooting explanation. Neither proves external actions occurred.</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </section>
    </div>
    <details class="landing-disclosure">
      <summary>What changes in a controlled variant?</summary>
      <p>Change one named fact: an expired certificate becomes a valid certificate. Keep the parent scenario and the exact before/after values. An expert explains why the expected behaviour changes, reviews the new version and compiles its checks. A synthetic variant does not replace real company evidence.</p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section class="landing-section landing-split" aria-labelledby="loop-title">
    <div>
      <h2 id="loop-title">Make the next failure a test</h2>
      <p>Support changes. Keep the test history without freezing your assumptions.</p>
    </div>
    <dl class="landing-loop">
      <div>
        <dt>Bring in a production failure</dt>
        <dd>Upload a structured trace and its reported correction. Inspect suggested scenario matches or create a draft. A match or imported correction cannot approve the expected behaviour.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Review, compile, retain</dt>
        <dd>An expert checks the evidence and approves a version. Grade the output, then deliberately add a failed case to regression. Stored replay grades an existing response; it does not rerun the production agent.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Compare like with like</dt>
        <dd>Compare saved runs on the same fixed case and visible input. Changed definitions stay unmatched. Source-change views flag linked evidence; they cannot discover every unrecorded assumption.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="landing-section" id="privacy" aria-labelledby="privacy-title">
    <div class="landing-section-heading">
      <h2 id="privacy-title">Your corpus is private input, not training material</h2>
      <p>Company support data can contain customer details, secrets and internal policies. Know what stays in the deployment and what a requested operation sends.</p>
    </div>
    <dl class="landing-boundaries">
      <div>
        <dt>Local by default</dt>
        <dd>Intake, local analysis, review and deterministic grading stay in your deployment. Workspace-scoped access separates company data. NavishAI does not train models on your corpus.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Explicit external disclosure</dt>
        <dd>Model-assisted source processing, HTTP targets and rubric judges need operator-approved endpoints and human disclosure confirmation. Targets receive visible context and permitted knowledge—not hidden expectations. Judges receive the fixed requirement, relevant evidence and output.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Masking has limits</dt>
        <dd>Email masking or authored exact-text rules do not remove all PII. Review retained titles, text and context before approving disclosure. Masking new input does not rewrite earlier snapshots.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Retention and deletion</dt>
        <dd>Source retention limits access. Source deletion also clears local derived scenarios, calibration and run records in that corpus. It cannot recall data already sent to an endpoint; remote retention belongs to that operator.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="landing-section landing-split" aria-labelledby="questions-title">
    <div>
      <h2 id="questions-title">Before you open the lab</h2>
      <p>A focused evaluation workflow, with clear limits.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="landing-questions">
      <details class="landing-disclosure">
        <summary>Is NavishAI a helpdesk or support agent?</summary>
        <p>No. NavishAI evaluates AI support systems. It does not run an inbox, draft customer replies or send customer messages. Historical conversations enter as evidence for tests.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="landing-disclosure">
        <summary>What data can I start with?</summary>
        <p>Upload supported Intercom or Zendesk JSON conversation exports, the lab's bounded conversation JSONL format, and text or Markdown documents. Structured production traces use the lab's trace schema. These are upload paths, not live vendor connections.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="landing-disclosure">
        <summary>How do I connect the system under test?</summary>
        <p>Use the lab's generic HTTPS target contract through an operator-approved public endpoint, including the incremental conversation contract when needed. Compatible stored outputs can be graded locally. The scripted adapter tests the machinery; it is not a live support agent. There are no built-in vendor agent integrations.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="landing-disclosure">
        <summary>Does a passing suite mean the agent is safe to ship?</summary>
        <p>It means the recorded outputs passed those fixed checks. It does not establish coverage of all company issues, grader accuracy or deployment readiness. Check expert disagreement and held-out evidence. Abstentions and execution errors stay distinct from behavioural failures; unknown cost stays unknown.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="landing-disclosure">
        <summary>Where does NavishAI run?</summary>
        <p>NavishAI supports a self-hosted, customer-controlled deployment. Operators own the database, TLS, secrets, jobs, backups and endpoint policy. Sign in to an existing deployment; first-Owner setup needs an active operator-issued bootstrap token. This page does not offer public account registration or a managed service.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="landing-close" aria-labelledby="close-title">
    <div>
      <h2 id="close-title">Start with the support decisions your AI must get right</h2>
      <p>Bring a slice of your support history and the agent you want to test. Your experts set the expectations; the failures worth keeping become your regression suite.</p>
      <p class="landing-note">Built by a Support leader with 13+ years in B2B SaaS post-sales. Questions? Write to <%= mail_to "hello@navishai.com" %>.</p>
    </div>
    <%= mail_to "hello@navishai.com", "Request a pilot", subject: "NavishAI pilot", class: "button button-primary" %>
  </section>

  <%= render "pages/footer", top_id: "landing-title" %>
</main>
